# The interface Vela ships (SCREENS.md §2.1).
#
# One module, so its screens can `use` each other — a `use` resolves in the declaring file (SCREENS.md
# §2.1) and this is the interface's file. Every project module can name these screens: a project's own
# declaration of a name takes it over, and the rest keep working.
#
# What is here grows as M12.2's items land: `game_menu` is the frame the app screens are built on, and
# the settings, save/load, history, help and notify screens follow. What an interface screen may rely on
# is deliberate and narrow: widget defaults and `theme.*` tokens of its own theme, and no `style` names,
# because a style resolves in the declaring module (SCREENS.md §5) and a project module using one of
# these would resolve the name against its own table. The palette is meant to be the
# project's — SCREENS.md §2.7 states it, and the injection that makes it true is not in yet, so this set
# draws in its own theme's colours today.

theme interface:
    color ink = 0xe8e8ee
    color panel = 0x14161d
    color accent = 0x5aa9e6

screen game_menu(title):
    box at = center, background = theme.panel:
        pad 32
        column gap = 16:
            text title
            transclude
            button:
                action close_screen()
                text "Return"
